          "changed": false,
          "titleChanged": false,
          "titleBefore": "Modbus TCP Server",
          "titleAfter": "Modbus TCP Server",
          "commentChanged": false,
          "commentBefore": "Serves this PLC's harness mirror to a Modbus TCP client. The server listens passively on TCP port 503 and accepts a connection from any client address, exposing 37 holding registers starting at MW1000 - the mirror the harness copy layer maintains, whose registers 0 and 1 are the build stamp, whose next two are the free-running scan counter, and whose tail carries the block-under-test observations and the harness violation latches. The span is the width the copy layer's register map needs rather than a round number, and it is widened whenever that map grows; counting signals does not give it, because a duration is 32 bits and so takes two registers. The exposed span is exactly the mirror: a register past the end returns an exception rather than a zero, so a client reading beyond what the copy layer maintains is told so instead of being handed a value that looks like data. Comms block - it carries no equipment logic and writes no plant tag. The holding-register area is given as an area pointer over marker memory, which is the form a register area in %M takes; it is bit memory rather than a data block because a data block can carry an optimized memory layout, which this server cannot read. DISCONNECT is taken from the server's own disconnect bit, which nothing in this program writes, so the connection stays up once made. Nothing here is retentive: the connection settings come back from their start values on every warm restart.",
          "commentAfter": "Serves this PLC's harness mirror to a Modbus TCP client. The server listens passively on TCP port 503 and accepts a connection from any client address, exposing 37 holding registers starting at MW1000 - the mirror the harness copy layer maintains, whose registers 0 and 1 are the build stamp, whose next two are the free-running scan counter, and whose tail carries the block-under-test observations and the harness violation latches. The span is the width the copy layer's register map needs rather than a round number, and it is widened whenever that map grows; counting signals does not give it, because a duration is 32 bits and so takes two registers. The exposed span is exactly the mirror: a register past the end returns an exception rather than a zero, so a client reading beyond what the copy layer maintains is told so instead of being handed a value that looks like data. Comms block - it carries no equipment logic and writes no plant tag. The holding-register area is given as an area pointer over marker memory, which is the form a register area in %M takes; it is bit memory rather than a data block because a data block can carry an optimized memory layout, which this server cannot read. DISCONNECT is taken from the server's own disconnect bit, which nothing in this program writes, so the connection stays up once made. Nothing here is retentive: the connection settings come back from their start values on every warm restart.",
